<h1 class="article-title">Partners of the Liepāja Sustainable Industry Centre Agree on Future Priorities</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>On 4 December 2024, partners of the Liepāja Sustainable Industry Centre gathered for their first joint working meeting to agree on practical cooperation and communication measures and develop an action plan for the near future. The objective is to strengthen energy independence and economic development in Liepāja, Latvia and the Baltic Sea region while meeting EU climate-neutrality and sustainability goals. The meeting took place at Riga Technical University (RTU), an important partner in scientific research, innovative technologies and education programmes preparing specialists for green energy and smart manufacturing.</p><p>On 12 November 2024, the partners signed a joint statement on strategically important private investment projects in green energy and smart manufacturing in Liepāja. The signatories agreed to establish the Liepāja Sustainable Industry Centre within the Liepāja Special Economic Zone.</p><p>The centre aims to become a major Baltic regional player in green energy and innovative technologies. Its members include Van Oord, Euroports, Smulders, CIS Liepāja, GI Termināls and Fokker Next Gen Latvia, together with the Liepāja SEZ Authority, Liepāja City Council, the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia (LIAA) and Riga Technical University.</p><p> </p><p>Planned projects:</p><ol><li>An offshore wind support base and heavy cargo terminal at the Port of Liepāja, providing infrastructure for building wind farms in the Baltic Sea and locating factories for large structures near the berths. Implemented by Van Oord, Euroports and Smulders.</li><li>A hydrogen production plant and terminal, producing hydrogen from renewable energy sources. Implemented by CIS Liepāja.</li><li>A sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) plant, producing fuel to help aviation transition towards more environmentally friendly energy sources. Implemented by GI Termināls.</li><li>Hydrogen-powered aircraft manufacturing, producing passenger aircraft at Liepāja Airport and contributing to the decarbonisation of aviation. Implemented by Fokker Next Gen Latvia.</li><li>A CO₂ export terminal for storing and transporting captured carbon dioxide emitted by industry and the energy sector. Implemented by GI Termināls.</li></ol><p>The joint statement aims to place these development projects at the top of the government agenda, securing state support through simplified procedures and approvals, access to financing instruments and infrastructure investment. The projects will promote Latvia’s economic sustainability, create new high-tech industries and contribute to GDP growth and industrial development.</p><p><i><strong> </strong></i></p><p>About the Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority:</p><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority provides a high-quality, attractive business environment and encourages development of the SEZ under unified management and clear conditions. It seeks to attract investors in smart, sustainable, high-value-added industries, particularly manufacturing, green energy and technology, creating skilled jobs with competitive pay.</p></div>
</div>
